      "text": "To find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\), we first need to determine the endpoints of \\( s_2 \\) by applying the given translation to the endpoints of \\( s_1 \\).\n\nThe endpoints of segment \\( s_1 \\) are \\((4, 1)\\) and \\((-8, 5)\\).\n\nThe translation is to move the segment 2 units to the right and 3 units up. Therefore, we add 2 to the x-coordinates and 3 to the y-coordinates of each endpoint.\n\nFor the endpoint \\((4, 1)\\):\n\\[\n4 + 2 = 6, \\quad 1 + 3 = 4\n\\]\nSo, the new coordinates are \\((6, 4)\\).\n\nFor the endpoint \\((-8, 5)\\):\n\\[\n-8 + 2 = -6, \\quad 5 + 3 = 8\n\\]\nSo, the new coordinates are \\((-6, 8)\\).\n\nThus, the endpoints of segment \\( s_2 \\) are \\((6, 4)\\) and \\((-6, 8)\\).\n\nNext, we find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\). The formula for the midpoint of a segment with endpoints \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the coordinates of the endpoints \\((6, 4)\\) and \\((-6, 8)\\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + (-6)}{2}, \\frac{4 + 8}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{0}{2}, \\frac{12}{2} \\right) = (0, 6)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{(0, 6)}\\).",
